<Base title="About the atlas">
  <div class="site-shell editorial-page">
    <div class="editorial-main">
      <p class="section-index">ABOUT / SIMUBOX</p>
      <h1>Scientific models, open to inspection.</h1>
      <p class="editorial-lede">A simulation can show what a model does. The equations, assumptions and source explain why it does it—and where the model stops.</p>
      <section><h2>A connected record</h2><p>Every entry connects a scientific topic to a model description, references, authors and source repository. Playable experiments add a browser workspace with controls. External candidates remain source-only while integration is reviewed.</p></section>
      <section><h2>Current scope</h2><p>The Lorenz attractor is the first original playable experiment. Mandelbrot Odyssey and Chaos Lab are indexed as external candidates. There are no accounts, uploads or automatic third-party builds in this release.</p></section>
      <section><h2>Origins</h2><p>Yahya (<a href="https://github.com/yahyabmo">yahyabmo</a>) contributed the initial conceptual research. The idea that this combination is useful is a product hypothesis; audience needs have not yet been validated through user research.</p></section>
    </div>
    <aside class="editorial-aside"><p class="section-index">READ THE ATLAS</p><h2>From observation to source</h2><ol><li>Choose a model by scientific field or topic.</li><li>Run it when a reviewed player is available.</li><li>Read its numerical method, limitations and references.</li><li>Inspect the source and attribution.</li></ol><a href="/simulations/">Explore models ↗</a></aside>
  </div>
</Base>
